  <section class="wrap fiche-body">
    <div>
      <h2>The vibe</h2>
      <p>The Gorges du Caramy stretch for about 4.5 km, from the Saut du Cabri (Mazaugues) to the Pont de Cassède, called the "Roman bridge" in Tourves but actually dating from the 15th century and listed as a Historic Monument since 1937. The site is Natura 2000 classified for its riverside woodland of alders, ash and poplars, a refuge for bats and birds. In 2025, the town and the Sainte-Baume regional nature park developed the entrance (car and bicycle parking spaces, dry toilets, restored shelter), created a discovery loop on both banks thanks to two fords and fenced off the fragile tufa and spring areas.</p>

      <h2>What to know before you go</h2>
      <p>The park's regulations are clear: dogs kept on a leash, droppings picked up, stay on the paths so as not to damage the banks, picnics tolerated as long as you take your rubbish away (bins in the car park). Swimming is not supervised and some marked areas are off-limits because of the tufa; boating, river hiking, dams in the river, fires, bivouacking and camping are banned. Visit Var also reports unstable stepping stones and a risk of collapse on the Mazaugues side: stay on the waymarked paths. The discovery trail is 3.4 km with 9 panels, and a climb leads to the Saint-Probace chapel; the site is not suitable for pushchairs and there is no phone signal beyond the car park.</p>
      <p>To get there, the park recommends the chemin du Stade (along the N7 from the Ferrages roundabout), then the Robert-Porro stadium and 2 km to the car park of about 40 spaces, at the entrance to the gorges. From October to May, access is free every day. From 15 June to 15 September, all vehicles are banned from driving up to the main car park; you need to park at the P1 or P2 car parks near the stadium, then walk about 2 km (30 minutes). From June to September, access to the massif is also regulated by the prefecture depending on fire risk: check the map published every evening at 6pm.</p>
      <div class="callout">Coco's tip: before heading off, stop by the Musée des Gueules Rouges in Tourves, which traces bauxite mining in the Var.</div>

      <h2>Nearby</h2>
      <p>In the area, keep your dog on a leash everywhere. Warning: Lac de Carcès, often cited in the region, is a drinking-water reservoir where swimming is banned, so don't plan on it for your dog.</p>
    </div>

